HALF A -- NEGATIVE CONTROL, FENCING OFF (the deliverable)
==============================================================================
  worker A (pid 17275) SIGSTOP'd inside its 2nd model call -- frozen, not killed (ps state='T'; T = stopped)
  chain at freeze: 5 checkpoints, tip step 3 id ...39aa1d86
  worker B took the thread over with invoke(None, config): ok=True fence=None
  worker A SIGCONT'd -- the zombie thaws and writes
  worker A finished: ok=True error=None

  Assertion 1 -- TIER 1, general to LangGraph + PostgresSaver and in no way
  specific to deepagents: the checkpoint chain FORKS and one worker's work is lost
  [PASS] two distinct checkpoints share one parent_checkpoint_id  -- parent ...39aa1d86 -> children ...ace9b9f2, ...8969f5c2
  [PASS] one child written by worker B, one by the thawed zombie A  -- B ...ace9b9f2 / A ...8969f5c2
  [PASS] BOTH workers believe they succeeded; neither saw an error
  [PASS] worker B's committed work -- its terminal checkpoint included -- is no longer reachable from the tip  -- 3 of B's 8 checkpoints orphaned; B's terminal checkpoint ...d6971f7d is one of them

  Assertion 2 -- TIER 2, specific to deepagents' DeltaChannel transcript:
  the surviving branch's message list is MALFORMED, not merely superseded
    both workers ran the SAME model task at the fork parent and both wrote messages there;
    rows actually stored: 1  task_ids=['669bfbbc']  tool_calls=['tc_B2']
    every tool_use id stored ANYWHERE in the thread (writes + blobs): ['tc_A1', 'tc_B2']
    every tool_result id stored anywhere:          ['tc_A1', 'tc_A2', 'tc_B2']
  [PASS] the zombie's AIMessage was not superseded, it was ANNIHILATED: nothing in the thread offers tc_A2, yet a tool_result answering it is durable  -- one messages row survives at the fork parent (B's, offering tc_B2); the AIMessage offering tc_A2 is in no write row and no blob, while A's ToolMessage for tc_A2 sits on A's branch -- reachable only if A ran the same task at the same checkpoint and its put_writes was dropped
    thread as read back from the tip:
    HumanMessage   go                           tool_calls=- tool_call_id=-
    AIMessage      looking up k1                tool_calls=['tc_A1'] tool_call_id=-
    ToolMessage    value-of-k1                  tool_calls=- tool_call_id=tc_A1
    AIMessage      worker B turn 2              tool_calls=['tc_B2'] tool_call_id=-
    ToolMessage    value-of-k2                  tool_calls=- tool_call_id=tc_A2
    AIMessage      final answer from A          tool_calls=- tool_call_id=-
  [PASS] the list read back from the tip contains a tool_result whose tool_use is nowhere in it  -- tool_result tool_call_id='tc_A2' has no matching tool_use anywhere before it in this message list
         + also, but NOT what the assertion above tests: tool_use id='tc_B2' was never answered by a tool_result
    worker B returned to its caller: 'final answer from B'
    the thread's tip now says:       'final answer from A'
    A plain StateGraph on MessagesState forks the chain identically but
    leaves each branch internally consistent -- see README 'Two tiers'.

  Assertion 3 -- is it unresumable? (measured and reported, NOT asserted)
    .next at the tip = ()  (LangGraph thinks the run finished)
    the probes below MUTATE the thread; they run after every check() above
    invoke(None, config) -> ACCEPTED, 6 messages (no model call: nothing pending)
    next user turn       -> RuntimeError: provider would reject this request: tool_result tool_call_id='tc_A2' has no matching tool_use anywhere before it in this message list
    Plainly: NOT mechanically unresumable -- LangGraph reads, replays and
    resumes the forked thread without complaint. What is destroyed is the
    thread's USABILITY at the next model call, durably. Shown with our own
    validator, not a real provider's 400 (no API key here).

  Assertion 4 -- the independent witness: deepagents' OWN repair middleware (measured, NOT asserted)
    the thread after that next-turn probe:
    HumanMessage   go                           tool_calls=- tool_call_id=-
    AIMessage      looking up k1                tool_calls=['tc_A1'] tool_call_id=-
    ToolMessage    value-of-k1                  tool_calls=- tool_call_id=tc_A1
    AIMessage      worker B turn 2              tool_calls=['tc_B2'] tool_call_id=-
    ToolMessage    Tool call lookup with id t   tool_calls=- tool_call_id=tc_B2
    ToolMessage    value-of-k2                  tool_calls=- tool_call_id=tc_A2
    AIMessage      final answer from A          tool_calls=- tool_call_id=-
    HumanMessage   continue                     tool_calls=- tool_call_id=-
    PatchToolCallsMiddleware.before_agent fired, rewrote the list with RemoveMessage(REMOVE_ALL_MESSAGES)
    and injected a synthetic ToolMessage for the dangling tc_B2: True
    that repair is committed to the thread, and the orphaned tool_result SURVIVED it: True
    So the framework itself classifies half the damage as needing repair,
    fixes that half, and cannot fix the other half.

